Hyperspectral images are composed of multiple spectral layers that capture a scene's various properties. Real-world applications often involve altering these images to remove noise or improve visibility, but existing methods typically require manual correction or are limited to specific types of manipulation. This research introduces an innovative approach to recognize and reverse unsupervised transformations within hyperspectral images. We propose a novel autoencoder architecture that integrates multiple spectral layers and features to automatically detect changes across all dimensions. Through experiments on synthetic and real-world hyperspectral images, our method demonstrates superior performance in alteration recognition and reversal compared to existing approaches. The code is available on GitHub for reproducibility. 

\textbf{Keywords:} Hyperspectral images, unsupervised alteration recognition, feature integration, deep convolutional autoencoders, alteration reversal. 

\textbf{Thesis Statement:} This research introduces an unsupervised method for identifying and reversing various alterations in hyperspectral images using a deep convolutional autoencoder that integrates spectral and feature layers. 

\textbf{Main Contributions:} 
1. We introduce an unsupervised approach to alteration recognition and reversal in hyperspectral images. 
2. Our method is the first to automatically detect changes across all dimensions of spectral and feature layers. 
3. Our deep convolutional autoencoder architecture is novel and outperforms existing methods. 
4. Our results show significant improvement over existing approaches on both synthetic and real-world hyperspectral images. 
5. Our code is available for reproducibility. 

\textbf{Background and Motivation:} 
Hyperspectral images are valuable for various applications due to their multiple spectral layers. However, real-world images are often corrupted by noise or poor visibility, requiring alteration recognition and reversal to utilize these images effectively. 

\textbf{Methodology:} 
Our approach involves using a deep convolutional autoencoder to automatically detect changes across all dimensions of spectral and feature layers. The architecture is composed of multiple spectral and feature layers that are integrated using feature integration modules. 

\textbf{Experimental Results:} 
Our results show significant improvement over existing approaches on both synthetic and real-world hyperspectral images. 

\textbf{Conclusion:} 
Our method is the first to automatically detect changes across all dimensions of spectral and feature layers without relying on supervision. The novel deep convolutional autoencoder architecture outperforms existing methods and demonstrates superior performance in alteration recognition and reversal. 

